state.update {\n                it.copy(\n                    busy = r.phase == Phase.RUNNING,\n                    progress = r.progress,\n                    status = r.status ?: it.status,   // hmm: if null keep existing\n                    inputUri = it.inputUri ?: r.inputUri,\n                    outputUri = r.outputUri,\n                    outputName = r.outputName,\n                )\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\nstatus: repository status is non-null during conversion; after detection the detection status should show. When phase != RUNNING and r.status is the last conversion status, that's fine. But detection sets status then repository doesn't emit (same state) → no overwrite. OK. But ordering: on collection r.status ?: it.status — r.status may be stale conversion status while detection just updated it.status → if the repository emits later (progress) it overwrites detection status — acceptable (during conversion).\n\ninputUri = it.inputUri ?: r.inputUri — restore after reopen.\n\nDetected trust: Detected(hz, confidence, trusted). detectTuning:\n```kotlin\nval r = TuningDetector.estimate(...)\nif (r != null) {\n    val hz = round(r.referenceHz*10)/10\n    val trusted = r.confidence >= MIN_DETECT_CONFIDENCE\n    _state.update { it.copy(detected=Detected(hz,r.confidence,trusted),\n        status = if (trusted) \"Detected A≈...\" else \"Tuning unclear: best guess A≈$hz Hz (confidence c), using 440\") \n    if (trusted) autoFill in MainActivity (existing guard)\n}\n```\nMainActivity: useDetectedBtn shown when `det != null && !det.trusted`; text `getString(R.string.use_guess, formatted)`; on click sets fromEdit and hides (appliedDetuneFor guard: set appliedDetuneFor = state.inputUri to hide). Also hide if From is already equal? Simpler: hide once applied or on new detection.\n\nWait: \"with a tap or button to use the guess\" — button approach is fine.\n\nstrings: use_guess \"Use A≈%1$s Hz instead\", notif_title \"432 Hz Converter\", notif_done? Use status text in notification.\n\nPlayer bind abBtn — unchanged.\n\nRestore fileInfo on reopen: in collect:\n```kotlin\nstate.inputUri?.let { u -> if (fileInfoShownUri != u) { fileInfoShownUri = u; if not currently displaying, set fileInfo from repository? } }\n```\nKeep it simple: when state.inputUri changes and != lastDisplayedUri and != pickedUri → update fileInfo to displayName(u) + \" · (restored)\". Hmm, calling displayName (contentResolver query) on the main thread is fine for small. Do it.\n\nTuningDetector sustain implementation — rewriting the estimate loop:\n\n```kotlin\nminHz: Double = 200.0,\nminSustain: Int = 3,\n\n// per frame:\nval peaks = ArrayList of Triple? Define a small local data class Peak(freq, mag, sustain)\nCurrent frame peak detection same; matching:\nfor (pk in cur) {\n    var best: PeakPrev? = null; bestDist = matchHz\n    for (pp in prev) { d = abs(pk.freq - pp.freq); if d < bestDist {best=pp; bestDist=d} }\n    pk.sustain = if (best != null) best.sustain + 1 else 1\n    if (pk.sustain >= minSustain) vote(weight = pk.mag)\n}\nprev = cur\nReset prev between windows.\n```\nmatchHz: 3 * binHz? For a pure sine stationary across hop frames, frequency is identical → matched. In a chord partials drift? no, stationary. Real music vibrato ±few cents is fine within 3 bins (~6Hz at 1.95Hz bin; at 48kHz binHz=5.86 → 3 bins = 17.6Hz — reasonable for vibrato).\n\nmatchHz = 2.5 * binHz? Make it 2*binHz.\n\nWeight: multiply by min(sustain, 8)? \"Prefer\" — gate is sufficient; keep weight = mag. Actually to favor long-sustained, scale weight by sustain.coerceAtMost(4.0)? Would overweight stationary partials equally anyway. Keep gate only — simpler and predictable for tests.\n\nBut noise floor: in noisy test frames, random local maxima below threshold get filtered; noise peaks above 2% of frame max exist and are random → not sustained → gated out.\n\nOne subtlety: the same random noise bin being a local max in 3 consecutive frames would match — unlikely if